**[Question 1] - What is the problem?**

Can a personalized federated learner use differentially private, client-side estimates of distribution shift and predictive uncertainty to select a stable client-specific personalization bound, while preserving a shared representation, improving worst-client performance under Non-IID and unbalanced data, and remaining interpretable and communication-efficient?

**[Question 2] - Why is it interesting and important?**

FedAvg learns one model and can be biased by label and quantity skew; FedProx stabilizes local optimization but does not provide genuine personalization. Moreau-envelope methods such as pFedMe, Ditto-style proximal personalization, FedPer, FedRep, first-order personalization, hypernetworks, and partial initialization improve client adaptation in different ways, but generally use a fixed personalization strength or expose an accuracy--communication--privacy trade-off that is not client adaptive. Recent work also shows that the value and depth of personalization are setting dependent, so personalizing every client equally is not necessarily optimal.

The proposed problem matters because deployed FL systems contain clients with very different sample sizes, shifts, privacy sensitivities, and reliability. A private adaptation controller could let a stable client reuse global knowledge while allowing a genuinely shifted client to specialize, improving both average utility and the accuracy floor without sending raw data or full personalized models. It would give the community a common way to study personalization strength as a measurable, privacy-constrained resource, and could support trustworthy applications such as mobile health, keyboards, and on-device vision. Reporting worst-client accuracy, calibration, and explanations alongside the mean could also shift pFL evaluation toward safer and fairer deployment.

**[Question 3] - Why is it hard?**

The shift and uncertainty signals are themselves computed from sensitive local examples, and noisy differentially private estimates can produce an inappropriate personalization bound. A client with few samples may look highly uncertain merely because its estimate has high variance; a client with noisy labels may be pushed toward overfitting; and a client with a genuinely new class may be incorrectly pulled toward the global model. Local objectives also conflict: aggressive personalization improves local accuracy but destroys transferable representations and can amplify client drift, while averaging personalized parameters is generally ill-defined.

The method must therefore couple three unstable processes: private statistic estimation, adaptive local optimization, and global representation aggregation. It must remain stable under client dropout and partial participation, bound updates under heterogeneous data sizes, and keep the extra controller state small enough that communication does not erase the benefit. Privacy noise also complicates fair comparisons because equal epsilon does not imply equal utility across clients. Finally, feature attributions or prototypes can leak information, so interpretability outputs need clipping, aggregation, or a privacy budget of their own.

**[Question 4] - Why hasn't it been solved before?**

Prior pFL work mostly chooses a fixed proximal coefficient, a fixed local/global parameter split, or a learned hypernetwork mapping; these choices do not reliably reflect per-client uncertainty, sample support, or privacy risk. FedProx and pFedMe/Ditto control optimization drift but do not privately infer when a client should personalize. FedPer and FedRep isolate parameters but leave the adaptation depth and representation transfer largely predetermined. Hypernetworks and partial initialization can increase flexibility or reduce transmission, yet their client descriptors and personalization decisions are not generally differentially private or accompanied by fairness guarantees. Differentially private FL commonly protects the aggregated global update, while overlooking leakage and instability in the client-specific adaptation signal.

The missing ingredient is a unified, auditable controller that (i) derives adaptation strength from privatized local evidence, (ii) separates globally shareable representations from local adapters, (iii) uses uncertainty-aware safeguards for small or noisy clients, and (iv) evaluates the resulting privacy--utility--fairness frontier. This proposal differs by treating personalization strength as a bounded decision variable rather than a single global hyperparameter. It also makes the decision reproducible through a released formula, privacy accounting, clipped summaries, and per-client explanation tests.

**[Question 5] - What are the key components of my approach and results?**

We will develop DP-ARD (Differentially Private Adaptive Representation Distillation). Each client receives a global encoder and classifier plus a small local adapter/head. During local training, it optimizes supervised loss plus a temperature-scaled distillation loss from the global encoder. It clips per-example gradients and applies DP-SGD. Without transmitting examples, the client computes clipped summaries of (a) prediction entropy/calibration error, (b) embedding drift from the received global model, (c) gradient disagreement, and (d) effective sample support. Gaussian noise is added locally; secure aggregation is used for any population statistic. A public formula maps the privatized summaries to a bound b_i in [b_min,b_max], with smaller b_i (and a smaller adapter learning rate) for uncertain, low-support clients and larger b_i for consistently shifted clients. The bound controls both the adapter update norm and the distillation weight, so it cannot silently become unconstrained local training. Only the shared encoder update and a compressed adapter state are communicated; noisy attribution/prototype summaries are optional and separately budgeted.

Experiments will use CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100 with 100 clients under Dirichlet label skew (alpha in {0.1, 0.5}), quantity skew, label-subset skew, and feature/rotation shift, plus FEMNIST with its natural writer heterogeneity. We will run repeated seeds with 10--20 clients per round and compare FedAvg, FedProx, pFedMe, Ditto, FedPer, FedRep, Per-FedAvg, hypernetwork/partial-initialization baselines, and private DP-FedAvg or private distillation variants. Ablations will remove privacy noise, replace adaptive b_i with a fixed bound, remove each signal, vary epsilon and delta, change adapter size and distillation temperature, and stress-test dropout, noisy labels, rare classes, and delayed statistics.

Primary metrics are mean client accuracy/F1, worst-client and bottom-20% accuracy, fairness gap (90th--10th percentile), global and unseen-class accuracy, calibration (ECE), robustness under shift and label noise, rounds to target accuracy, transmitted bytes and energy proxy, and formal per-client/global (epsilon, delta) accounting. We expect DP-ARD to retain or improve mean accuracy while raising the worst-client floor and reducing fairness gaps relative to fixed-strength pFL, with lower communication than full-model personalization. The main result will be a measured Pareto frontier showing when adaptive personalization is worth its privacy and communication cost, together with sensitivity analyses that identify failure regimes rather than claiming universal gains.
